course: dictionary
lesson: 1168
title: Broad: wide, general, and open
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: «Broad» means wide, from one side to the other. In «A platypus has a broad flat tail», the tail is wide, not long or thin. Test it by swapping in wide: the sentence still makes sense, so you know you have the right meaning.
explain: Broad can also mean general rather than detailed. A «broad idea» or «broad topic» covers the big picture without every small step. When someone says «broadly speaking», they are giving the main points, not every detail.
explain: The words next to broad show you which meaning to use. «In broad daylight» means openly, in full daylight, so a thief who steals in broad daylight is bold and could be seen. «A broad accent» means a strong accent that is easy to notice. Always read the whole phrase before you decide.
fact: Broad means wide from one side to the other.
fact: «Broad» hooves are wide, which helps reindeer walk on snow and ice.
fact: Broad can mean general, covering many things rather than narrow or detailed.
fact: «In broad daylight» means openly, in full daylight.
fact: A «broad accent» is strong and easy to notice.
exercise: «A platypus has a broad flat tail.» What does broad mean here? | Long and thin | *Wide from one side to the other | Hidden in fur | Quiet and soft
exercise: Ellie says a «broad idea» means a very detailed plan. What does broad idea mean? | A wide river | A very detailed plan with every step written down | *A general idea that covers the big picture | A secret idea
exercise: «The thief ran off in broad daylight.» What does this tell you? | He hid in a cupboard | He ran away at night | He was too small to be seen | *He was seen openly, in full daylight
test: «Her broad smile lit up the room.» What does broad mean here? | Quiet and hidden | Thin and small | Sad and tired | *Wide, a big smile across the face
test: Dad says, «Broadly speaking, the trip was fun.» What does broadly speaking tell you? | He is shouting loudly | He is talking about one small detail only | He is speaking exactly, to the last minute | *He is giving the general picture, not every detail
exam: Which meaning fits «He spoke with a broad accent that everyone could hear»? | *Strong and easy to notice | Wide from side to side | Openly, in full daylight | General, not detailed
